The DHCP reader: every scope read, the lease list kept here, the inventory sent signed

WO-0928-B item 7. A -collect-dhcp mode that binds to MS-DHCPM exactly as the
probe does (the bind is extracted from run() into bindDHCP, unchanged, so the
two cannot come to differ), reads every page of every scope, reduces the
leases in memory to what R20 permits, and posts the result to the portal's
collection door signed with the appliance key (reportRun's signing extracted
into signedPost, the same way).

What leaves: one item per hardware address, carrying the address and the
hostname as the server holds it (R19(2)). Coverage leaves as three counts. No
IP, no scope, no subnet, no expiry, no lease state -- those are the lease
list. Last seen is the collection time, because a lease records when it
expires and working back from that is a date computed from a duration (0.3).
A lease whose hardware address cannot be read travels as an empty item, so
the portal counts it as it already counts one.

The hardware address is accepted only in the three shapes MS-DHCPM 2.2.1.2.5
names, each checked against the same record: six bytes; seven starting 0x01;
eleven whose first four equal the client's own address ANDed with its mask,
then 0x01. Anything else is refused and counted.

NOT RUN AGAINST A DHCP SERVER. The parse follows the specification's worked
example and has not met a live lease; the probe's own note that the lease
path is proven to connect and not to parse still stands. No binary is
committed: see the WO-0928-B report on how a binary reaches the box.

